WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.430
- [Narrator] China
imported over $5 billion

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.140
of U.S. corn last year

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.540
even though it's the world's
second largest producer

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.740
of the crop.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.170
The world's top producer is the U.S.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.300
And most of the corn these
countries are producing

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.310
isn't even eaten by people.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.940
About 75% of China's
corn and 40% of the U.S.'

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.620
is used for animal feed.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.660
Livestock get fatter
when they're fed corn,

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.130
and China's growing middle class

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.380
is eating more and more meat.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.350
China now needs about
250 million metric tons

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:29.790
of corn per year.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.580
So much corn that it has
to import millions of tons

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.020
from other countries.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.660
And now China has begun
working to cut its reliance

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.380
on foreign corn,
particularly from the U.S.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.830
- Any country that's trying
to maintain livestock,

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.610
be it cows, pigs, what have
you, they need to have corn.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.890
- [Narrator] But China's farms
like far behind the U.S.,

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.440
which is a major concern
for its food security.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.880
Here's why growing its own grains

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.560
has been such a struggle for China

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.140
and how it's been trying to
wean itself off U.S. corn,

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.490
which could leave a significant gap

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:01.023
in America's export market.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.290
U.S. farmers are much more
successful at growing corn

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.910
than their counterparts in China.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:07.800
The U.S. department of
agriculture estimates

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:08.850
that China's corn fields

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.520
yielded less than three
tons per acre last year

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.530
versus nearly five in the U.S.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.580
- It's widely believed from
the folks that I talked to

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.940
that the U.S. has the superior soil,

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:24.210
the superior farming techniques.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.310
- [Narrator] The U.S. has
significantly more arable land

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.560
than China, and the arable
land that China does have

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.080
is decreasing due to urbanization.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.360
China's soil is also heavily polluted.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.630
A 2014 report found nearly 1/5
of the country's arable land

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.880
was contaminated with heavy metals.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.310
China is currently conducting
a national soil survey

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.380
to see the extent of the issue.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.240
A second key advantage for U.S. farmers

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:46.830
is their growing practices.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.030
To try and grow more,

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.640
Chinese farmers use massive
amounts of fertilizer,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.830
more than four times the global average.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.560
Many Chinese farmers are
using fertilizer inefficiently

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.380
and polluting the larger
ecosystem in the process.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.150
A third issue for Chinese farmers,

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.300
their seeds are about 20
years behind U.S. technology.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.390
Many in China are suspicious
of genetically modified crops,

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:07.440
but the government has worked

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.360
to overcome that skepticism.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.500
Even in its recent push

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.420
to embrace genetically modified corn,

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.000
China only allowed a tiny
fraction of its corn fields

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.130
to be planted with
genetically modified seeds,

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.750
according to reports.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.270
So China has turned to the U.S. for corn.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:22.590
While its banned domestic farmers

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.210
from growing genetically modified corn

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.620
due to public opposition,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.870
China has still imported
genetically modified corn

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.070
to feed animals.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.070
But as tensions have escalated
between the two countries,

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.300
China has put more effort

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.330
into finding alternative trade
partners to get the resource,

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:37.980
adding competition to the market.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.370
- They're in the process of setting up

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.830
kind of their own trade network

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:48.600
that can support their
population and their economy,

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:53.600
and do so in a way where
they're free of U.S. influence,

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.480
free of Western influence in general.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.550
- [Narrator] Namely, this
means turning to Brazil,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.200
which is right behind the U.S. and China

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.060
when it comes to corn production.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.550
- For China, they're in
the process of seeking out

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:08.910
the countries that can
handle their demand,

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.300
and which is why Brazil is a key partner

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.890
that they're developing a
stronger partnership with.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.620
It's because Brazil is able to grow

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.500
as a corn-producing country,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.380
and they see that growth as supportive

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.150
for their demand going forward.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:27.983
- [Narrator] For a long time,

00:03:27.983 --> 00:03:30.390
Brazil was known for having
a volatile corn supply,

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.400
but the country's now
producing far more corn

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.340
than ever before, increasing
how many acres are planted

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:36.990
and growing a second crop of corn

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.480
after farmers harvest soybeans.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.490
It hasn't had to do everything on its own.

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.020
Foreign businesses have been investing

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.120
in Brazil's agriculture for decades.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.800
Chinese companies have spent billions

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.110
to get involved in the
growing Latin American market

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:52.320
and everything from seeds to
processing and transportation.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.050
Things have shifted even further
since the war in Ukraine.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:57.720
For years, China was getting
lots of its corn there.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.420
Ukraine is one of the world's
largest corn producers,

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.550
but its corn production
has fallen significantly

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.140
in the past few years.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.910
The war has made it harder for farmers

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:08.310
to get financing for corn
seeds and fertilizer,

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:09.900
and some farmland has been unusable

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.790
due to occupation or mines.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:15.030
So China has doubled down on
its relationship with Brazil.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:16.470
Last year, China signed an agreement

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.650
to allow significantly more
corn imports from the country.

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:22.380
The result, Brazil may surpass the U.S.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:23.940
in corn exports this year.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:27.540
- Brazil is receiving increased
market share of exports

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.000
that used to be in the U.S.' domain.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.740
There's competitions for prices,

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.260
who has the most competitive prices,

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.270
and Brazil often wins that.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.950
- [Narrator] Because Brazil
still has lots of room

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.960
to expand its corn growing acreage,

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.360
it could soon surpass the
U.S. in corn production.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.490
- There's a lot of room to grow,

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:45.630
and that's what they're doing.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:47.040
They're aggressively growing.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.930
- [Narrator] This could
pose significant competition

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:51.690
for the U.S., especially in
light of other circumstances

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.277
facing the country's corn market.

00:04:53.277 --> 00:04:55.530
The U.S. is currently
facing a trade dispute

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.260
with its second biggest
corn customer, Mexico,

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.210
which plans to ban
genetically modified corn

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.280
by the end of 2023.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.810
The country says this is to preserve

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:06.390
heirloom varieties of corn
that grow domestically,

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:09.570
but it's a big deal since more
than 90% of U.S. grown corn

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:11.040
is genetically modified.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.380
- Export markets are always a top concern.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:16.350
If China is no longer purchasing

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.440
or exports in general are to drop off,

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.840
then the price that
farmers are gonna receive

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.210
at the grain elevators for their crop

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.380
have the potential to sink.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.170
For farmers that are already operating

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:31.830
on really thin margins,
that's very difficult.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:35.868
And a lot of times when
this kind of thing happens,

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:38.970
there's some farmers that
simply cannot overcome that.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.100
- [Narrator] U.S. farmers could
potentially sell more corn

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.440
to ethanol plants that
could use it to make fuel,

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:44.820
but losing the export market

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:47.040
could significantly lower prices.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.608
- They wouldn't be able to
cover the amount of volume

00:05:49.608 --> 00:05:53.516
that China and exports in general take up

00:05:53.516 --> 00:05:55.800
from the U.S. producers.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.660
They wouldn't be able to cover that

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.070
just through ethanol alone.

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:00.780
- [Narrator] Ultimately,
China aims to grow

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.520
all the basic grains it needs,

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.160
whether that's corn,
wheat, rice, or soybeans.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:06.660
But corn is just one crop

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.700
where China's crop production falls short.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.310
The country also relies
on foreign soybeans.

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:15.090
It imported over $16 billion
worth from the U.S. last year.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.400
Soybeans and corn often
grow on the same land,

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.080
but corn is more productive.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:20.940
China's average soybean yield per acre

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.390
is less than 1/3 that of
corn, and is about 40% less

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:26.280
than the United States'
average soybean yield

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:27.960
for the same plot.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.210
Some Chinese researchers
have been urging farmers

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:31.620
to adopt new planting methods

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:34.380
that could allow them to
grow both soy and corn,

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.730
but they worry farmers prefer

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:37.830
to just stick with what
they're already doing.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.930
Experts believe China
could grow self-sufficient

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:41.940
in corn production by
the end of the decade.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.310
The recombination of
better planting practices,

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:46.620
using cornbread to maximize growth,

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:48.450
and improving the soil quality.

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:50.610
But that would require
embracing new technology

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:52.170
and farmer education.

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:53.880
- They would have to
grow significantly more

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.600
in order to be self-sufficient
the way that the U.S. is,

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.870
and they would have a
long way to go to do that.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:02.100
- [Narrator] For the foreseeable future,

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:03.960
China will probably need to keep importing

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.060
large amounts of corn if
it wants to keep using it

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:07.350
in animal feed,

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:10.410
but soon, that may no longer
mean buying from the U.S.

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:11.947
- China is in the position

00:07:11.947 --> 00:07:15.180
where they don't have
enough to feed their people,

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:17.970
which is why they import from outside.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.640
And they've been reliant on us

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.370
as the primary corn producer in the world

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.250
even if they really don't
want to do business with us.

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.740
That's why they're
building trade agreements

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.710
that you can essentially
go around the U.S.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:35.370
The U.S. doesn't really
have an alternative to China

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:39.090
in terms of the buying
power that they have.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:40.350
- [Narrator] Farmers
have seen what can happen

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:43.200
when China stops buying
U.S. agricultural goods.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.640
When China sharply reduced orders

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.340
for products like corn
and soybeans in 2018,

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.530
farms hit record levels of debt

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:52.680
despite the U.S. government
responding with billions in aid.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.570
If China stops buying us corn again

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:56.940
and moves its business
to countries like Brazil,

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.040
farmers would need to find a replacement.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:02.250
But having to replace a
$5 billion export market

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:03.540
could be a major problem

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.038
for farmers who are already
getting buy on thin margins.

00:08:07.038 --> 00:08:10.205
(gentle upbeat music)

